    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Topography results from the competition between the endogenic tectonic processes of uplift (isostacy) and deformation, and the exogenic geomorphic processes of erosion, transport, and sedimentation. Tectonic landforms are erosive remnants of tectonic features such as fault scarps or relatively uplifted and deformed blocks. The subtleties of these landforms hold information concerning the tectonic history of of a particular region. This report reviews both previous and proposed investigations of the tectonic landforms in a semi-arid region of complex,active, distributed deformation: the area along the San Andreas Fault (SAF) in the southern Carrizo Plain and the Elkhorn Hills, San Luis Obispo County, California (Figure 1). Careful description of the structure and topography of this actively deforming region will be used to constrain a mechanical analysis that will in turn, provide an understanding of the tectonic history and erosive modification of the area.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
